1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:07,890
Listen.

2
00:00:08,530 --> 00:00:09,050
Practice.

3
00:00:09,570 --> 00:00:09,910
Speak.

4
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:12,230
Practice makes you perfect.

5
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:17,650
Hello everyone and welcome back to Office English Daily.

6
00:00:18,050 --> 00:00:18,750
I'm Ryan.

7
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:19,830
And I'm Chloe.

8
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:23,000
We're so happy you decided to practice English with us today.

9
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:30,050
If you want to sound more natural and confident in an office environment, today's lesson is going to help you a lot.

10
00:00:30,240 --> 00:00:30,880
That's right.

11
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:37,210
Today we're going to have real English conversations that people actually use in offices every single day.

12
00:00:37,690 --> 00:00:50,070
We'll talk about meetings, teamwork, deadlines, emails, presentations, office problems, lunch breaks, office gossip, promotions, and everyday communication with coworkers.

13
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:56,330
And we'll do everything through natural conversations so you can learn practical English in a realistic way.

14
00:00:56,390 --> 00:00:57,510
Exactly.

15
00:00:57,530 --> 00:01:00,530
Don't worry if you don't understand every word immediately.

16
00:01:00,750 --> 00:01:04,520
Just relax, listen carefully, and enjoy the conversation.

17
00:01:05,140 --> 00:01:09,100
The more you listen to English conversations, the easier speaking becomes.

18
00:01:09,660 --> 00:01:10,360
All right, Chloe.

19
00:01:10,690 --> 00:01:12,350
Let's begin our office day.

20
00:01:12,870 --> 00:01:13,450
Sounds good.

21
00:01:13,830 --> 00:01:15,710
Let's start with the morning at the office.

22
00:01:16,230 --> 00:01:17,150
Good morning, Chloe.

23
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:18,180
Morning, Ryan.

24
00:01:18,550 --> 00:01:19,910
You're here really early today.

25
00:01:19,950 --> 00:01:23,650
Yeah, I wanted to finish a report before everyone arrived.

26
00:01:24,110 --> 00:01:25,840
Wow, you're motivated this morning.

27
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:28,420
Honestly, I didn't have much choice.

28
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:30,220
The deadline is this afternoon.

29
00:01:30,960 --> 00:01:32,090
Ah, that explains it.

30
00:01:32,630 --> 00:01:33,190
What about you?

31
00:01:33,230 --> 00:01:34,730
How was your commute today?

32
00:01:35,210 --> 00:01:35,910
Terrible.

33
00:01:36,250 --> 00:01:38,740
The train was delayed for almost 20 minutes.

34
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:39,880
Seriously?

35
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:42,820
Yeah, and the station was completely crowded.

36
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:44,620
That sounds stressful.

37
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:45,760
It really was.

38
00:01:46,140 --> 00:01:47,830
I thought I was going to be late for work.

39
00:01:48,290 --> 00:01:49,930
Well, at least you made it on time.

40
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:51,020
Barely.

41
00:01:51,320 --> 00:01:53,300
I was practically running into the building.

42
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:56,270
That explains why you look exhausted already.

43
00:01:56,750 --> 00:01:57,130
Thanks.

44
00:01:57,250 --> 00:01:58,070
Very kind of you.

45
00:01:58,110 --> 00:01:59,700
I'm just being honest.

46
00:02:00,260 --> 00:02:01,920
So, how was your weekend?

47
00:02:02,070 --> 00:02:03,350
Pretty relaxing, actually.

48
00:02:03,690 --> 00:02:05,220
I stayed home most of the time.

49
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:06,520
That sounds nice.

50
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:11,660
Yeah, I watched movies, cleaned my apartment, and cooked a few new recipes.

51
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:14,830
Look at you being productive outside the office, too.

52
00:02:15,510 --> 00:02:16,030
I try.

53
00:02:16,050 --> 00:02:20,420
I spent most of my weekend helping my sister move into her new apartment.

54
00:02:20,580 --> 00:02:21,970
That must have been tiring.

55
00:02:22,450 --> 00:02:23,390
Extremely.

56
00:02:23,770 --> 00:02:26,130
I carried boxes for two days straight.

57
00:02:26,570 --> 00:02:28,900
You deserve extra coffee this morning, then.

58
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:30,240
Absolutely.

59
00:02:30,740 --> 00:02:33,610
Speaking of coffee, do you want me to grab you one from the kitchen?

60
00:02:34,130 --> 00:02:34,470
Really?

61
00:02:34,690 --> 00:02:35,690
That would be amazing.

62
00:02:36,210 --> 00:02:36,460
Sure.

63
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:37,300
What do you want?

64
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:39,280
Just a latte with no sugar.

65
00:02:39,780 --> 00:02:40,240
Got it.

66
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:41,320
Thanks, Ryan.

67
00:02:41,830 --> 00:02:42,510
No problem.

68
00:02:42,770 --> 00:02:46,060
Hey, Ryan, before you go, did you check your emails yet?

69
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:46,960
Not yet.

70
00:02:47,280 --> 00:02:47,520
Why?

71
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:51,210
The manager sent an important message about today's client meeting.

72
00:02:51,710 --> 00:02:52,130
Oh, really?

73
00:02:52,590 --> 00:02:53,270
Yeah.

74
00:02:53,290 --> 00:02:56,410
Apparently, the client requested a few last-minute changes.

75
00:02:56,940 --> 00:02:57,720
You're kidding.

76
00:02:58,300 --> 00:02:59,300
I wish I was.

77
00:02:59,860 --> 00:03:00,200
All right.

78
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:02,140
I should probably read that immediately.

79
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:03,160
Definitely.

80
00:03:03,660 --> 00:03:04,670
Good morning, everyone.

81
00:03:05,170 --> 00:03:05,990
Morning, Olivia.

82
00:03:06,430 --> 00:03:07,170
How are you today?

83
00:03:07,210 --> 00:03:09,430
Honestly, pretty stressed.

84
00:03:10,180 --> 00:03:10,510
Uh-oh.

85
00:03:10,530 --> 00:03:11,080
What happened?

86
00:03:11,430 --> 00:03:15,560
I accidentally sent the wrong spreadsheet to the finance department yesterday.

87
00:03:15,620 --> 00:03:16,800
Oh, no.

88
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:19,640
Yeah, I realized the mistake this morning.

89
00:03:20,040 --> 00:03:20,820
Did they notice?

90
00:03:21,440 --> 00:03:22,390
Not yet, I think.

91
00:03:22,830 --> 00:03:23,950
Well, that's good at least.

92
00:03:24,470 --> 00:03:27,920
I already sent the corrected version along with an apology email.

93
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:29,500
That was the right thing to do.

94
00:03:30,020 --> 00:03:31,640
I still feel embarrassed, though.

95
00:03:32,060 --> 00:03:33,450
Don't be too hard on yourself.

96
00:03:33,890 --> 00:03:35,510
Everyone makes mistakes at work.

97
00:03:35,730 --> 00:03:38,450
I know, but I hate making careless mistakes.

98
00:03:38,910 --> 00:03:43,340
Trust me, people care more about how you fix mistakes than the mistakes themselves.

99
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:45,260
That's actually reassuring.

100
00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:51,140
Besides, compared to some office disasters, sending the wrong spreadsheet is pretty minor.

101
00:03:51,660 --> 00:03:52,650
I guess that's true.

102
00:03:52,710 --> 00:03:58,170
Once, I accidentally emailed confidential meeting notes to the entire department.

103
00:03:58,670 --> 00:03:59,710
Wait, seriously?

104
00:03:59,750 --> 00:04:00,970
Yep.

105
00:04:00,990 --> 00:04:01,740
What happened?

106
00:04:02,020 --> 00:04:04,760
My manager called me into his office immediately.

107
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:06,630
That sounds terrifying.

108
00:04:07,190 --> 00:04:08,410
It absolutely was.

109
00:04:08,870 --> 00:04:09,690
Were you in trouble?

110
00:04:10,190 --> 00:04:10,990
Luckily, no.

111
00:04:11,370 --> 00:04:16,840
He understood it was an accident, but I definitely learned to double-check email recipients after that.

112
00:04:17,380 --> 00:04:19,530
Wow, that makes me feel a little better.

113
00:04:19,990 --> 00:04:20,250
Good.

114
00:04:20,750 --> 00:04:23,010
Office mistakes happen to everyone eventually.

115
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:24,540
Excuse me, Ryan.

116
00:04:24,620 --> 00:04:25,820
Do you have a minute?

117
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:26,360
Sure, Jake.

118
00:04:26,540 --> 00:04:26,880
What's up?

119
00:04:27,420 --> 00:04:32,710
I'm trying to prepare the presentation slides, but I'm confused about one part of the sales data.

120
00:04:33,130 --> 00:04:33,830
Which section?

121
00:04:34,290 --> 00:04:35,830
The quarterly revenue chart.

122
00:04:36,330 --> 00:04:37,290
Let me take a look.

123
00:04:37,750 --> 00:04:37,970
Here.

124
00:04:38,770 --> 00:04:39,880
Ah, I see the issue.

125
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:40,800
What's wrong?

126
00:04:41,280 --> 00:04:45,020
You accidentally used last year's numbers instead of this year's data.

127
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:46,060
Oh, wow.

128
00:04:46,260 --> 00:04:47,400
I didn't even notice that.

129
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:49,230
It's an easy mistake to make.

130
00:04:49,650 --> 00:04:50,550
Thanks for catching it.

131
00:04:51,030 --> 00:04:51,990
No problem.

132
00:04:52,090 --> 00:04:56,940
Honestly, there's so much information in these reports that my brain feels overloaded.

133
00:04:56,960 --> 00:04:58,200
I understand.

134
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:00,830
Preparing presentations can be stressful.

135
00:05:01,310 --> 00:05:04,360
Especially when the client meeting is only a few hours away.

136
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:05,620
Exactly.

137
00:05:05,870 --> 00:05:08,960
By the way, how do you stay calm during presentations?

138
00:05:09,460 --> 00:05:09,900
Practice.

139
00:05:10,420 --> 00:05:10,840
That's it?

140
00:05:11,220 --> 00:05:11,740
Pretty much.

141
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:15,370
The more presentations you give, the more comfortable you become.

142
00:05:15,870 --> 00:05:18,530
I always get nervous before speaking in front of people.

143
00:05:18,990 --> 00:05:19,750
Most people do.

144
00:05:19,770 --> 00:05:20,620
Really?

145
00:05:20,640 --> 00:05:21,860
Absolutely.

146
00:05:22,280 --> 00:05:25,140
Even experienced professionals get nervous sometimes.

147
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:26,790
That's surprising.

148
00:05:26,870 --> 00:05:31,930
The trick is learning how to manage the nervousness instead of trying to eliminate it completely.

149
00:05:32,390 --> 00:05:33,210
That makes sense.

150
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:35,640
Also, preparation helps a lot.

151
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:36,400
True.

152
00:05:36,820 --> 00:05:40,230
When you know your material well, you naturally feel more confident.

153
00:05:40,670 --> 00:05:43,530
I should probably rehearse my section one more time then.

154
00:05:43,990 --> 00:05:44,510
Good idea.

155
00:05:45,030 --> 00:05:45,870
All right, everyone.

156
00:05:45,910 --> 00:05:47,740
The morning meeting is about to begin.

157
00:05:47,800 --> 00:05:49,600
Let's head to the conference room.

158
00:05:50,100 --> 00:05:51,300
Did you bring your laptop?

159
00:05:51,720 --> 00:05:51,940
Yep.

160
00:05:52,320 --> 00:05:53,140
Ready to go.

161
00:05:53,290 --> 00:05:54,230
Good morning, team.

162
00:05:54,290 --> 00:05:55,760
Thank you all for joining.

163
00:05:56,300 --> 00:05:56,620
Morning.

164
00:05:57,080 --> 00:06:03,670
Today we need to discuss project updates, deadlines, and preparations for the client presentation this afternoon.

165
00:06:03,730 --> 00:06:05,060
Sounds good.

166
00:06:05,260 --> 00:06:06,600
Let's start with marketing.

167
00:06:06,860 --> 00:06:08,460
Chloe, could you give us an update?

168
00:06:08,920 --> 00:06:09,200
Sure.

169
00:06:09,780 --> 00:06:12,930
Our social media campaign performed very well this week.

170
00:06:13,410 --> 00:06:13,770
Great.

171
00:06:14,250 --> 00:06:17,920
We increased engagement by 15% compared the last month.

172
00:06:18,460 --> 00:06:19,460
Excellent progress.

173
00:06:19,980 --> 00:06:24,050
We also gained nearly 5,000 new followers across all platforms.

174
00:06:24,570 --> 00:06:25,410
That's impressive.

175
00:06:25,910 --> 00:06:30,120
However, we noticed lower performance on email campaigns.

176
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:31,240
Any idea why?

177
00:06:31,280 --> 00:06:35,810
We think the subject lines may not have been strong enough to attract attention.

178
00:06:36,430 --> 00:06:36,810
I see.

179
00:06:36,870 --> 00:06:41,340
We're currently testing different approaches to improve open rates.

180
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:42,120
Good strategy.

181
00:06:42,560 --> 00:06:45,230
We should have updated results by next week.

182
00:06:45,250 --> 00:06:45,710
Perfect.

183
00:06:45,910 --> 00:06:46,650
Thank you, Chloe.

184
00:06:47,030 --> 00:06:47,750
You're welcome.

185
00:06:47,930 --> 00:06:50,780
Ryan, how's the product launch preparation going?

186
00:06:51,320 --> 00:06:53,100
Overall, things are moving smoothly.

187
00:06:53,540 --> 00:06:54,100
Good to hear.

188
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:59,790
The design team finalized the packaging yesterday, and production starts next Monday.

189
00:07:00,350 --> 00:07:00,850
Excellent.

190
00:07:01,310 --> 00:07:04,190
We're still waiting for final approval from legal, though.

191
00:07:04,690 --> 00:07:06,130
Is that going to delay anything?

192
00:07:06,620 --> 00:07:07,480
Hopefully not.

193
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:08,400
All right.

194
00:07:08,540 --> 00:07:09,580
Please keep us updated.

195
00:07:10,040 --> 00:07:10,600
Of course.

196
00:07:10,870 --> 00:07:14,210
Does anyone have concerns or questions before we continue?

197
00:07:14,230 --> 00:07:15,810
Actually, I do.

198
00:07:16,390 --> 00:07:16,870
Go ahead.

199
00:07:17,390 --> 00:07:20,540
I'm worried that the current timeline may be too aggressive.

200
00:07:21,060 --> 00:07:21,700
What do you mean?

201
00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:25,520
Several departments are already overloaded with work.

202
00:07:25,540 --> 00:07:26,160
That's true.

203
00:07:26,760 --> 00:07:31,310
If we continue adding urgent tasks, productivity could actually decrease.

204
00:07:31,790 --> 00:07:33,050
That's a valid concern.

205
00:07:33,530 --> 00:07:35,550
Maybe we should reconsider some deadlines.

206
00:07:36,130 --> 00:07:36,630
I agree.

207
00:07:36,690 --> 00:07:38,370
We don't want the team burning out.

208
00:07:38,890 --> 00:07:39,470
Exactly.

209
00:07:39,820 --> 00:07:42,390
Let's review the project schedule after lunch.

210
00:07:42,930 --> 00:07:43,490
Sounds good.

211
00:07:43,990 --> 00:07:44,830
All right, everyone.

212
00:07:45,110 --> 00:07:47,470
Let's take a short break before the next discussion.

213
00:07:48,070 --> 00:07:50,360
Finally, my brain needed that break.

214
00:07:50,940 --> 00:07:51,580
Same here.

215
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:54,160
Hey, Chloe, want to grab coffee downstairs?

216
00:07:54,600 --> 00:07:55,160
Definitely.

217
00:07:55,700 --> 00:07:57,840
So what do you think about the new manager so far?

218
00:07:58,340 --> 00:07:59,810
Honestly, I like her.

219
00:08:00,330 --> 00:08:00,630
Really?

220
00:08:01,110 --> 00:08:03,470
Yeah, she's demanding, but fair.

221
00:08:03,450 --> 00:08:04,050
I agree.

222
00:08:04,610 --> 00:08:07,690
She also communicates clearly, which makes a huge difference.

223
00:08:08,300 --> 00:08:09,060
Absolutely.

224
00:08:09,360 --> 00:08:12,480
Bad communication creates so many office problems.

225
00:08:13,040 --> 00:08:13,660
Exactly.

226
00:08:14,310 --> 00:08:18,030
I once worked for a manager who gave extremely vague instructions.

227
00:08:18,470 --> 00:08:19,630
That sounds frustrating.

228
00:08:20,150 --> 00:08:20,990
It was awful.

229
00:08:21,360 --> 00:08:23,200
Nobody knew what he actually wanted.

230
00:08:23,660 --> 00:08:26,180
Then probably people made mistakes constantly.

231
00:08:26,800 --> 00:08:27,580
All the time.

232
00:08:28,060 --> 00:08:31,330
That's why clear communication is so important in the workplace.

233
00:08:31,810 --> 00:08:32,510
Definitely.

234
00:08:32,660 --> 00:08:35,180
By the way, did you hear about Sarah's promotion?

235
00:08:35,610 --> 00:08:36,210
Yeah, I did.

236
00:08:36,270 --> 00:08:38,110
She totally deserves it.

237
00:08:38,570 --> 00:08:39,290
Absolutely.

238
00:08:39,530 --> 00:08:41,140
She works incredibly hard.

239
00:08:41,640 --> 00:08:44,800
I've never seen someone manage so many projects at once.

240
00:08:45,420 --> 00:08:45,750
Same.

241
00:08:46,230 --> 00:08:48,390
And she always stays calm under pressure.

242
00:08:48,810 --> 00:08:51,100
That's a valuable skill in office environments.

243
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:53,940
Especially during stressful deadlines.

244
00:08:54,110 --> 00:08:54,750
Exactly.

245
00:08:55,190 --> 00:08:56,670
Hey guys, can I join you?

246
00:08:57,150 --> 00:08:57,810
Of course, Sarah.

247
00:08:58,150 --> 00:08:59,930
Congratulations on your promotion.

248
00:08:59,970 --> 00:09:01,190
Thanks so much.

249
00:09:01,740 --> 00:09:02,740
You must be excited.

250
00:09:03,260 --> 00:09:04,880
I am, but I'm nervous too.

251
00:09:05,420 --> 00:09:06,120
Why nervous?

252
00:09:06,600 --> 00:09:09,200
Because leading a team is a big responsibility.

253
00:09:09,660 --> 00:09:09,940
True.

254
00:09:10,480 --> 00:09:13,350
I want to do a good job and support everyone properly.

255
00:09:13,870 --> 00:09:14,850
I think you'll be great.

256
00:09:15,350 --> 00:09:16,730
Thanks, I appreciate that.

257
00:09:17,130 --> 00:09:19,610
Do you know when you officially start the new position?

258
00:09:20,090 --> 00:09:20,750
Next Monday.

259
00:09:21,290 --> 00:09:22,710
Wow, that's soon.

260
00:09:22,880 --> 00:09:23,280
I know.

261
00:09:23,340 --> 00:09:24,560
Things are moving quickly.

262
00:09:25,020 --> 00:09:27,130
Are you going to change anything in the department?

263
00:09:27,630 --> 00:09:28,450
Not immediately.

264
00:09:28,950 --> 00:09:29,750
Smart decision.

265
00:09:29,810 --> 00:09:33,260
First, I want to understand everyone's workflow better.

266
00:09:33,300 --> 00:09:35,400
That's probably the best approach.

267
00:09:35,900 --> 00:09:38,510
Then maybe I'll suggest improvements gradually.

268
00:09:39,030 --> 00:09:40,250
Sounds very professional.

269
00:09:40,850 --> 00:09:41,370
I hope so.

270
00:09:41,910 --> 00:09:43,020
Excuse me, everyone.

271
00:09:43,060 --> 00:09:45,680
The client meeting starts in 15 minutes.

272
00:09:45,930 --> 00:09:47,790
All right, time to focus.

273
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:49,280
Is everyone ready?

274
00:09:49,880 --> 00:09:51,320
As ready as I'll ever be.

275
00:09:51,780 --> 00:09:53,120
Don't worry, you'll do great.

276
00:09:53,580 --> 00:09:53,880
Thanks.

277
00:09:54,380 --> 00:09:55,550
Good afternoon, everyone.

278
00:09:55,870 --> 00:09:57,650
Thank you for visiting our office today.

279
00:09:58,090 --> 00:09:59,410
Thank you for inviting us.

280
00:09:59,930 --> 00:10:01,440
Please make yourselves comfortable.

281
00:10:01,860 --> 00:10:02,220
Thanks.

282
00:10:02,780 --> 00:10:05,380
Would anyone like coffee or tea before we begin?

283
00:10:05,860 --> 00:10:06,830
Coffee would be great.

284
00:10:07,290 --> 00:10:07,690
Of course.

285
00:10:08,230 --> 00:10:10,570
All right, shall we begin the presentation?

286
00:10:10,550 --> 00:10:11,930
Yes, let's get started.

287
00:10:12,340 --> 00:10:16,500
Today, we're excited to present our strategy for your upcoming campaign.

288
00:10:17,020 --> 00:10:19,110
We're looking forward to hearing your ideas.

289
00:10:19,730 --> 00:10:20,150
Excellent.

290
00:10:20,570 --> 00:10:25,420
First, we analyzed your company's current market position and customer engagement levels.

291
00:10:26,060 --> 00:10:26,600
Interesting.

292
00:10:27,020 --> 00:10:30,930
Based on our research, we identified several opportunities for growth.

293
00:10:31,410 --> 00:10:32,810
Could you explain that further?

294
00:10:33,310 --> 00:10:33,770
Certainly.

295
00:10:34,210 --> 00:10:39,280
Your social media presence is strong, but your content schedule lacks consistency.

296
00:10:39,880 --> 00:10:40,300
I see.

297
00:10:40,360 --> 00:10:45,160
We believe more frequent posting would significantly improve audience engagement.

298
00:10:45,580 --> 00:10:46,370
That makes sense.

299
00:10:46,850 --> 00:10:52,150
Additionally, short-form videos currently perform very well across most platforms.

300
00:10:52,270 --> 00:10:53,970
We've noticed that trend too.

301
00:10:54,450 --> 00:10:54,750
Great.

302
00:10:55,210 --> 00:10:59,440
We also recommend increasing collaboration with influencers in your industry.

303
00:10:59,940 --> 00:11:01,890
Why influencers specifically?

304
00:11:02,330 --> 00:11:05,750
Because they already have strong relationships with your target audience.

305
00:11:06,220 --> 00:11:06,920
Good point.

306
00:11:07,100 --> 00:11:09,140
This approach can increase brand trust.

307
00:11:09,160 --> 00:11:09,990
Hello, friends.

308
00:11:10,250 --> 00:11:13,830
Welcome back to another English listening practice podcast.

309
00:11:14,250 --> 00:11:14,770
I'm Daniel.

310
00:11:15,130 --> 00:11:15,890
And I'm Emma.

311
00:11:16,320 --> 00:11:19,000
We're so happy you're practicing English with us today.

312
00:11:19,360 --> 00:11:22,010
Today, we have a very useful topic for you.

313
00:11:22,550 --> 00:11:25,630
Real English conversations in an office.

314
00:11:25,610 --> 00:11:26,150
That's right.

315
00:11:26,630 --> 00:11:34,230
If you work in an office, want to work in an office, or simply want to improve your professional English, this lesson is perfect for you.

316
00:11:34,570 --> 00:11:39,630
You'll hear natural conversations that people use every single day at work.

317
00:11:39,650 --> 00:11:47,260
We'll talk about meetings, emails, deadlines, teamwork, presentations, office problems, coffee breaks, and much more.

318
00:11:47,710 --> 00:11:50,350
And don't worry if your English is still improving.

319
00:11:50,690 --> 00:11:54,550
We use simple and clear English so you can learn naturally.

320
00:11:54,590 --> 00:11:58,050
All right, everyone, let's begin today's Office English podcast.

321
00:11:58,370 --> 00:11:59,170
Good morning, Emma.

322
00:11:59,410 --> 00:12:00,590
You look busy already.

323
00:12:00,970 --> 00:12:01,650
Morning, Daniel.

324
00:12:01,990 --> 00:12:05,280
Yeah, I'm trying to finish this report before the manager arrives.

325
00:12:05,760 --> 00:12:06,740
Is it due today?

326
00:12:06,760 --> 00:12:08,480
Unfortunately, yes.

327
00:12:08,980 --> 00:12:10,600
I stayed up last night working on it.

328
00:12:11,040 --> 00:12:12,280
That sounds exhausting.

329
00:12:12,680 --> 00:12:13,540
It really was.

330
00:12:13,950 --> 00:12:14,910
I barely slept.

331
00:12:15,370 --> 00:12:17,390
Well, at least you're almost finished now.

332
00:12:17,790 --> 00:12:18,110
True.

333
00:12:18,650 --> 00:12:21,350
I just need to double-check the numbers one more time.

334
00:12:21,450 --> 00:12:22,830
That's always a good idea.

335
00:12:23,210 --> 00:12:26,460
Small mistakes in reports can cause big problems later.

336
00:12:26,900 --> 00:12:27,600
Exactly.

337
00:12:28,040 --> 00:12:30,640
I'd rather spend extra time checking everything carefully.

338
00:12:31,120 --> 00:12:31,810
Smart move.

339
00:12:32,410 --> 00:12:35,170
By the way, did you see the email from Mr. Collins?

340
00:12:35,550 --> 00:12:36,010
Which one?

341
00:12:36,390 --> 00:12:38,960
He sends about 20 emails every morning.

342
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,860
The one about the client meeting this afternoon.

343
00:12:42,400 --> 00:12:42,940
Oh, yes.

344
00:12:43,080 --> 00:12:43,560
I saw that.

345
00:12:43,980 --> 00:12:46,010
The meeting starts at 2 p.m., right?

346
00:12:46,020 --> 00:12:46,700
Exactly.

347
00:12:47,020 --> 00:12:51,120
And apparently the client has a lot of questions about the project timeline.

348
00:12:51,460 --> 00:12:51,900
Great.

349
00:12:52,300 --> 00:12:55,510
That probably means the meeting will take longer than expected.

350
00:12:55,710 --> 00:12:56,430
Most likely.

351
00:12:56,770 --> 00:12:59,830
Honestly, I don't mind meetings if they're productive.

352
00:13:00,330 --> 00:13:00,910
Same here.

353
00:13:01,270 --> 00:13:05,360
The problem is when people repeat the same points again and again.

354
00:13:05,380 --> 00:13:09,480
Oh yes, some meetings could honestly just be emails.

355
00:13:09,980 --> 00:13:11,150
I completely agree.

356
00:13:11,210 --> 00:13:13,790
Anyway, are you ready for your presentation today?

357
00:13:14,230 --> 00:13:15,150
Not completely.

358
00:13:15,190 --> 00:13:18,370
I'm still nervous about speaking in front of the client.

359
00:13:18,390 --> 00:13:18,650
Really?

360
00:13:18,700 --> 00:13:21,480
You always seem confident during presentations.

361
00:13:21,700 --> 00:13:24,800
Trust me, I still get nervous every single time.

362
00:13:24,840 --> 00:13:26,040
That's surprising.

363
00:13:26,060 --> 00:13:29,430
I think most people feel nervous before presentations.

364
00:13:29,830 --> 00:13:30,670
Probably true.

365
00:13:30,710 --> 00:13:33,720
I still get nervous answering phone calls sometimes.

366
00:13:34,160 --> 00:13:34,480
Really?

367
00:13:34,520 --> 00:13:37,420
Especially when the client speaks very quickly.

368
00:13:38,280 --> 00:13:39,830
Ah, I understand that feeling.

369
00:13:40,250 --> 00:13:43,870
Sometimes I need people to repeat themselves two or three times.

370
00:13:43,990 --> 00:13:45,170
There's nothing wrong with that.

371
00:13:45,510 --> 00:13:49,370
It's better to ask questions than misunderstand important information.

372
00:13:49,730 --> 00:13:50,270
That's true.

373
00:13:50,790 --> 00:13:56,180
Actually, one of the best phrases I learned at work was, could you clarify that for me?

374
00:13:56,620 --> 00:13:58,020
That's a very useful phrase.

375
00:13:58,480 --> 00:14:00,580
It sounds professional and polite.

376
00:14:00,600 --> 00:14:01,140
Exactly.

377
00:14:01,160 --> 00:14:05,310
I also use, just to confirm, you mean quite often.

378
00:14:05,870 --> 00:14:06,330
Oh, yes.

379
00:14:06,430 --> 00:14:07,950
That's another excellent phrase.

380
00:14:08,390 --> 00:14:09,950
It helps avoid confusion later.

381
00:14:09,990 --> 00:14:11,190
Definitely.

382
00:14:11,240 --> 00:14:14,920
Miscommunication can create huge problems in office work.

383
00:14:15,330 --> 00:14:17,270
Especially when deadlines are involved.

384
00:14:17,730 --> 00:14:21,410
Speaking of deadlines, how many projects are you handling right now?

385
00:14:21,750 --> 00:14:23,220
Honestly, too many.

386
00:14:23,760 --> 00:14:24,340
Same here.

387
00:14:24,800 --> 00:14:30,790
I currently have four active projects, and every client thinks their project is the most important one.

388
00:14:31,210 --> 00:14:32,730
That's classic office life.

389
00:14:33,210 --> 00:14:33,950
Exactly.

390
00:14:33,990 --> 00:14:36,700
Everyone says their request is urgent.

391
00:14:37,260 --> 00:14:39,560
I hate when everything becomes urgent.

392
00:14:39,790 --> 00:14:40,490
Me too.

393
00:14:40,530 --> 00:14:44,540
If everything is urgent, then nothing is truly urgent anymore.

394
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:45,600
Very true.

395
00:14:45,980 --> 00:14:53,030
Yesterday, I received an email marked extremely urgent, and it turned out to be about changing the font color in a document.

396
00:14:53,490 --> 00:14:54,130
Seriously?

397
00:14:54,170 --> 00:14:55,630
I'm not even joking.

398
00:14:56,080 --> 00:14:57,120
That's unbelievable.

399
00:14:57,520 --> 00:14:57,920
I know.

400
00:14:57,940 --> 00:15:00,140
I almost laughed when I opened the email.

401
00:15:00,600 --> 00:15:02,970
Office life can be ridiculous sometimes.

402
00:15:03,510 --> 00:15:04,230
Absolutely.

403
00:15:04,730 --> 00:15:07,250
So, do you have any plans after work today?

404
00:15:07,320 --> 00:15:09,560
Honestly, I'm going straight home.

405
00:15:09,980 --> 00:15:11,160
I'm exhausted this week.

406
00:15:11,660 --> 00:15:12,040
Same.

407
00:15:12,100 --> 00:15:16,350
I canceled dinner plans yesterday because I was too tired to socialize.

408
00:15:16,770 --> 00:15:18,230
I completely understand.

409
00:15:18,690 --> 00:15:20,850
Sometimes work drains all your energy.

410
00:15:21,310 --> 00:15:23,700
Especially during busy seasons like this.

411
00:15:24,200 --> 00:15:24,860
Exactly.

412
00:15:24,900 --> 00:15:27,900
I feel like my brain never stops working lately.

413
00:15:28,400 --> 00:15:28,960
Same here.

414
00:15:29,020 --> 00:15:32,630
Even when I'm at home, I keep thinking about unfinished tasks.

415
00:15:33,050 --> 00:15:35,550
That's one of the hardest things about office jobs.

416
00:15:35,720 --> 00:15:37,780
Learning how to disconnect from work?

417
00:15:38,220 --> 00:15:39,000
Exactly.

418
00:15:39,520 --> 00:15:41,650
I'm still trying to improve that myself.

419
00:15:42,090 --> 00:15:42,590
Me too.

420
00:15:42,950 --> 00:15:45,030
Hey, Emma, can I help you with something quickly?

421
00:15:45,400 --> 00:15:45,840
Of course.

422
00:15:46,040 --> 00:15:46,520
What do you need?

423
00:15:46,980 --> 00:15:52,270
I'm reviewing this email before sending it to the client, and I want to make sure it sounds professional.

424
00:15:52,630 --> 00:15:52,850
Sure.

425
00:15:52,890 --> 00:15:53,650
Let me take a look.

426
00:15:54,090 --> 00:15:54,530
Thanks.

427
00:15:54,750 --> 00:15:55,610
Hmm.

428
00:15:55,630 --> 00:15:57,720
Honestly, it looks pretty good already.

429
00:15:58,200 --> 00:15:58,460
Really?

430
00:15:58,860 --> 00:15:59,180
Yes.

431
00:15:59,720 --> 00:16:01,860
I would just change this sentence slightly.

432
00:16:02,260 --> 00:16:02,760
Which part?

433
00:16:03,120 --> 00:16:09,850
Instead of saying, send me the information quickly, I'd say, could you please send the information as soon as possible?

434
00:16:10,630 --> 00:16:12,870
Ah, that definitely sounds more professional.

435
00:16:13,270 --> 00:16:13,810
Exactly.

436
00:16:14,310 --> 00:16:17,660
Small wording changes can make emails sound much more polite.

437
00:16:18,080 --> 00:16:18,620
Good point.

438
00:16:19,080 --> 00:16:21,700
Also, maybe add a thank you sentence at the end.

439
00:16:21,850 --> 00:16:24,420
Something like, thank you for your cooperation?

440
00:16:24,740 --> 00:16:25,280
Perfect.

441
00:16:25,700 --> 00:16:26,600
Thanks for helping me.

442
00:16:26,980 --> 00:16:27,540
No problem.

443
00:16:27,900 --> 00:16:30,070
You're always good at writing professional emails.

444
00:16:30,520 --> 00:16:30,890
Thanks.

445
00:16:31,350 --> 00:16:33,930
I used to struggle a lot with business English though.

446
00:16:34,330 --> 00:16:34,630
Really?

447
00:16:34,670 --> 00:16:35,510
Oh, yes.

448
00:16:35,970 --> 00:16:39,700
When I first started working here, writing emails took me forever.

449
00:16:40,220 --> 00:16:41,180
I know that feeling.

450
00:16:41,540 --> 00:16:45,130
I constantly worried about grammar mistakes and sounding professional.

451
00:16:45,570 --> 00:16:46,130
Same here.

452
00:16:46,550 --> 00:16:49,290
But over time, it became easier with practice.

453
00:16:49,510 --> 00:16:50,390
That's encouraging.

454
00:16:50,710 --> 00:16:52,830
Honestly, repetition helps a lot.

455
00:16:53,340 --> 00:16:58,040
Office communication becomes easier when you use the same professional phrases regularly.

456
00:16:58,560 --> 00:16:59,220
Very true.

457
00:16:59,620 --> 00:17:02,030
Hey, Emma, are you joining us for lunch later?

458
00:17:02,370 --> 00:17:02,870
Probably.

459
00:17:02,930 --> 00:17:04,210
Where are you all going?

460
00:17:04,630 --> 00:17:08,230
There's a new cafe across the street that everyone wants to try.

461
00:17:08,730 --> 00:17:09,190
Oh, yes.

462
00:17:09,320 --> 00:17:10,320
I heard about that place.

463
00:17:10,720 --> 00:17:12,900
Apparently, their pasta is amazing.

464
00:17:13,280 --> 00:17:14,760
Then I'm definitely coming.

465
00:17:15,180 --> 00:17:15,520
Great.

466
00:17:15,760 --> 00:17:17,530
We're leaving around 1.30pm.

467
00:17:17,610 --> 00:17:18,170
Sounds good.

468
00:17:18,230 --> 00:17:20,610
I should finish my presentation before then.

469
00:17:21,010 --> 00:17:21,710
Good luck with that.

470
00:17:22,030 --> 00:17:22,350
Thanks.

471
00:17:22,590 --> 00:17:23,110
I'll need it.

472
00:17:23,490 --> 00:17:25,600
Are you still making changes to the slides?

473
00:17:26,060 --> 00:17:27,340
Just small adjustments.

474
00:17:27,840 --> 00:17:32,080
Sometimes I spend too much time trying to make presentations perfect.

475
00:17:32,460 --> 00:17:32,920
Me too.

476
00:17:33,380 --> 00:17:38,310
I'll spend 30 minutes changing tiny design details nobody will even notice.

477
00:17:38,790 --> 00:17:40,230
That's called perfectionism.

478
00:17:40,710 --> 00:17:41,830
And it's exhausting.

479
00:17:42,410 --> 00:17:43,390
Very exhausting.

480
00:17:43,830 --> 00:17:47,390
I'm trying to improve my time management skills because of that.

481
00:17:47,460 --> 00:17:48,060
Same here.

482
00:17:48,640 --> 00:17:50,560
What usually helps you stay organized?

483
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:54,070
I make task lists every morning before starting work.

484
00:17:54,570 --> 00:17:55,430
Oh, I do that too.

485
00:17:55,470 --> 00:17:58,950
It helps me prioritize tasks and reduce stress.

486
00:17:59,390 --> 00:17:59,990
Exactly.

487
00:18:00,010 --> 00:18:03,440
Otherwise, I feel overwhelmed by everything I need to do.

488
00:18:03,960 --> 00:18:04,340
Same.

489
00:18:04,780 --> 00:18:07,060
Do you use a digital planner or a notebook?

490
00:18:07,580 --> 00:18:10,380
Actually, I still prefer writing things by hand.

491
00:18:10,760 --> 00:18:11,020
Really?

492
00:18:11,060 --> 00:18:11,760
Yeah.

493
00:18:12,180 --> 00:18:15,190
Writing tasks physically helps me remember them better.

494
00:18:15,290 --> 00:18:15,930
Interesting.

495
00:18:16,450 --> 00:18:17,770
I use apps for everything.

496
00:18:18,230 --> 00:18:21,280
I guess both methods work as long as you stay organized.

497
00:18:21,630 --> 00:18:22,160
Very true.

498
00:18:22,680 --> 00:18:23,200
Oh, no.

499
00:18:23,260 --> 00:18:24,260
What happened?

500
00:18:24,660 --> 00:18:29,090
I just realized I forgot to attach the file in my email to the client.

501
00:18:29,470 --> 00:18:29,930
Again?

502
00:18:29,970 --> 00:18:31,810
Unfortunately, yes.

503
00:18:32,250 --> 00:18:32,730
Don't worry.

504
00:18:33,090 --> 00:18:34,850
Everyone forgets attachments sometimes.

505
00:18:35,380 --> 00:18:37,180
I know, but it's still embarrassing.

506
00:18:37,580 --> 00:18:39,120
Just send a quick follow-up email.

507
00:18:39,580 --> 00:18:42,740
Something like, please find the attachment included?

508
00:18:42,910 --> 00:18:43,490
Exactly.

509
00:18:43,850 --> 00:18:44,950
Short and professional.

510
00:18:45,280 --> 00:18:45,900
Good idea.

511
00:18:45,920 --> 00:18:49,060
Trust me, clients see this mistake all the time.

512
00:18:49,460 --> 00:18:51,010
That actually makes me feel better.

513
00:18:51,390 --> 00:18:51,630
Good.

514
00:18:52,010 --> 00:18:54,630
Hey, Emma, can I ask you an opinion about something?

515
00:18:55,030 --> 00:18:55,270
Sure.

516
00:18:55,670 --> 00:18:58,780
Do you think remote work is better than working in the office?

517
00:18:59,180 --> 00:19:02,610
Honestly, I think both have advantages and disadvantages.

518
00:19:03,030 --> 00:19:03,670
That's fair.

519
00:19:03,850 --> 00:19:08,480
Working from home is comfortable, but sometimes I miss interacting with coworkers.

520
00:19:08,960 --> 00:19:09,500
Same here.

521
00:19:09,980 --> 00:19:13,240
Also, staying focused at home could be difficult sometimes.

522
00:19:13,780 --> 00:19:14,480
Very true.

523
00:19:14,720 --> 00:19:16,350
There are too many distractions.

524
00:19:16,830 --> 00:19:17,530
Exactly.

525
00:19:17,970 --> 00:19:21,970
Laundry suddenly becomes very interesting when you're trying to work.

526
00:19:21,990 --> 00:19:23,750
That's hilarious, but accurate.

527
00:19:24,110 --> 00:19:24,670
What about you?

528
00:19:24,730 --> 00:19:25,730
Which do you prefer?

529
00:19:26,180 --> 00:19:27,780
I actually like hybrid work.

530
00:19:28,240 --> 00:19:29,320
A combination of both?

531
00:19:29,820 --> 00:19:30,400
Exactly.

532
00:19:30,480 --> 00:19:34,210
A few days at home and a few days in the office feels balanced to me.

533
00:19:34,610 --> 00:19:35,630
I completely agree.

534
00:19:36,130 --> 00:19:39,230
It gives flexibility while still maintaining teamwork.

535
00:19:39,250 --> 00:19:42,360
Plus, some meetings are simply easier in person.

536
00:19:42,380 --> 00:19:42,920
Definitely.

537
00:19:43,320 --> 00:19:45,820
Communication feels more natural face-to-face.

538
00:19:46,300 --> 00:19:48,260
Especially brainstorming sessions.

539
00:19:48,700 --> 00:19:49,320
Exactly.

540
00:19:49,790 --> 00:19:53,350
Creative discussions work better when everyone is physically together.

541
00:19:53,690 --> 00:19:55,390
Alright, should we head to lunch now?

542
00:19:55,640 --> 00:19:56,480
Absolutely.

543
00:19:56,720 --> 00:19:57,480
I'm starving.

544
00:19:57,900 --> 00:19:58,400
Same here.

545
00:19:58,850 --> 00:20:00,210
I skipped breakfast this morning.

546
00:20:00,590 --> 00:20:02,590
That's dangerous during busy work days.

547
00:20:03,050 --> 00:20:03,430
I know.

548
00:20:03,830 --> 00:20:05,950
My stomach has been complaining for hours.

549
00:20:06,390 --> 00:20:08,520
Let's go before the cafe gets crowded.

550
00:20:08,880 --> 00:20:09,400
Good idea.

551
00:20:09,760 --> 00:20:11,620
Wow, this place is really nice.

552
00:20:12,080 --> 00:20:14,640
Yeah, the atmosphere feels very relaxing.

553
00:20:15,080 --> 00:20:16,500
Much calmer than the office.

554
00:20:16,900 --> 00:20:17,520
Definitely.

555
00:20:17,560 --> 00:20:19,290
So, what are you ordering?

556
00:20:19,710 --> 00:20:21,870
I think I'll try the chicken pasta.

557
00:20:21,930 --> 00:20:22,570
Good choice.

558
00:20:22,970 --> 00:20:23,500
What about you?

559
00:20:23,560 --> 00:20:25,600
Probably the grilled sandwich.

560
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:29,450
Honestly, lunch breaks are one of my favorite parts of office life.

561
00:20:29,930 --> 00:20:30,330
Same.

562
00:20:30,790 --> 00:20:33,460
It's nice having time to talk casually with coworkers.

563
00:20:33,960 --> 00:20:34,480
Exactly.

564
00:20:34,800 --> 00:20:36,590
It helps build better relationships.

565
00:20:37,050 --> 00:20:40,520
Good teamwork often starts with simple conversations like this.

566
00:20:40,750 --> 00:20:41,530
Very true.

567
00:20:41,990 --> 00:20:45,240
By the way, have you heard the rumor about the company restructuring?

568
00:20:45,660 --> 00:20:46,100
A little.

569
00:20:46,420 --> 00:20:48,630
People have been talking about it all week.

570
00:20:48,650 --> 00:20:49,610
Do you think it's true?

571
00:20:49,650 --> 00:20:51,590
Honestly, I'm not sure.

572
00:20:51,650 --> 00:20:54,020
Office rumors spread very quickly.

573
00:20:54,380 --> 00:20:55,020
That's true.

574
00:20:55,420 --> 00:20:58,150
Half the time the information is completely wrong.

575
00:20:58,300 --> 00:20:59,140
Exactly.

576
00:20:59,420 --> 00:21:03,450
That's why I try not to worry until management confirms something officially.

577
00:21:03,850 --> 00:21:04,550
Smart approach.

578
00:21:04,930 --> 00:21:07,670
Gossip usually creates unnecessary stress.

579
00:21:08,050 --> 00:21:08,610
Definitely.

580
00:21:09,070 --> 00:21:11,680
So, how long have you been working at this company now?

581
00:21:12,080 --> 00:21:13,120
Almost three years.

582
00:21:13,580 --> 00:21:15,260
Wow, time really flies.

583
00:21:15,740 --> 00:21:16,180
It does.

584
00:21:16,660 --> 00:21:18,510
I still remember my first day here.

585
00:21:18,890 --> 00:21:19,590
Were you nervous?

586
00:21:20,030 --> 00:21:21,370
Extremely nervous.

587
00:21:21,930 --> 00:21:22,310
Same.

588
00:21:22,750 --> 00:21:26,820
I barely spoke during my first week because I was afraid of making mistakes.

589
00:21:27,050 --> 00:21:29,220
I understand that feeling completely.

590
00:21:29,580 --> 00:21:32,020
But everyone turned out to be very supportive.

591
00:21:32,460 --> 00:21:33,900
That makes a huge difference.

592
00:21:34,300 --> 00:21:34,880
Definitely.

593
00:21:34,920 --> 00:21:38,490
Friendly co-workers can make stressful jobs much easier.

594
00:21:39,010 --> 00:21:39,710
Absolutely.

595
00:21:40,110 --> 00:21:40,430
All right.

596
00:21:40,650 --> 00:21:42,900
We should probably head back to the office soon.

597
00:21:43,500 --> 00:21:44,820
Unfortunately, yes.

598
00:21:45,160 --> 00:21:46,720
Reality is waiting for us.

599
00:21:47,220 --> 00:21:49,510
Along with hundreds of unread emails.

600
00:21:49,970 --> 00:21:51,390
Please don't remind me.

601
00:21:51,470 --> 00:21:52,910
Welcome back to Office Life.

602
00:21:53,470 --> 00:21:54,240
All right, everyone.

603
00:21:54,660 --> 00:21:58,240
Thank you so much for listening and practicing English with us today.

604
00:21:58,640 --> 00:22:12,380
We hope these office conversations helped you improve your listening skills and learn useful professional... Hello, everyone, and welcome back to the English at Work podcast, where we help you understand real, natural English used in everyday office life.

605
00:22:12,750 --> 00:22:13,470
Hi, everyone.

606
00:22:13,510 --> 00:22:15,060
Thanks for joining us again.

607
00:22:15,080 --> 00:22:19,240
Today's episode is a full, detailed office simulation.

608
00:22:19,280 --> 00:22:23,770
We're going to follow real conversations from morning to evening in a workplace.

609
00:22:24,410 --> 00:22:24,950
Exactly.

610
00:22:25,410 --> 00:22:29,440
Think of this like spending a full day in an English-speaking office.

611
00:22:29,460 --> 00:22:36,330
You'll hear greetings, meetings, emails, deadlines, teamwork, problems, and even workplace pressure.

612
00:22:36,600 --> 00:22:40,860
Just listen carefully and try to imagine yourself in these situations.

613
00:22:41,380 --> 00:22:43,920
Let's begin with the start of the day at the office.

614
00:22:44,520 --> 00:22:45,420
Good morning, Lisa.

615
00:22:45,440 --> 00:22:46,230
You're early today.

616
00:22:46,730 --> 00:22:47,510
Good morning, Mark.

617
00:22:47,930 --> 00:22:50,510
Yes, I wanted to prepare before the team meeting.

618
00:22:50,950 --> 00:22:52,950
I feel like we have a lot to cover today.

619
00:22:53,490 --> 00:22:54,500
We definitely do.

620
00:22:55,080 --> 00:22:57,600
Did you check the project updates from the client last night?

621
00:22:58,100 --> 00:22:58,460
I did.

622
00:22:58,520 --> 00:23:03,130
There are a few changes we need to discuss, especially in the budget section.

623
00:23:03,410 --> 00:23:04,450
I saw that too.

624
00:23:04,950 --> 00:23:06,730
It might affect our timeline slightly.

625
00:23:07,320 --> 00:23:07,900
Exactly.

626
00:23:07,940 --> 00:23:10,280
I think we should bring it up in the meeting first.

627
00:23:10,960 --> 00:23:11,460
Good idea.

628
00:23:11,800 --> 00:23:14,330
Let's not make any decisions before everyone is aligned.

629
00:23:14,870 --> 00:23:15,230
Agreed.

630
00:23:15,670 --> 00:23:17,270
By the way, how was your evening?

631
00:23:17,790 --> 00:23:18,390
Pretty quiet.

632
00:23:18,730 --> 00:23:21,840
I tried to disconnect from work, but I still ended up checking emails.

633
00:23:22,540 --> 00:23:24,770
That sounds like every office worker's life.

634
00:23:25,550 --> 00:23:25,790
True.

635
00:23:25,850 --> 00:23:28,370
All right, let's get ready for the meeting.

636
00:23:28,540 --> 00:23:29,600
Good morning, everyone.

637
00:23:29,940 --> 00:23:31,160
Let's start today's meeting.

638
00:23:31,540 --> 00:23:34,160
First, I want to go through the project status.

639
00:23:35,020 --> 00:23:38,970
Sorry to interrupt, but before you begin, can I clarify the agenda?

640
00:23:39,430 --> 00:23:39,850
Of course.

641
00:23:40,110 --> 00:23:40,490
Go ahead.

642
00:23:41,150 --> 00:23:45,090
Are we focusing more on the client feedback or internal deadlines today?

643
00:23:45,690 --> 00:23:46,270
Good question.

644
00:23:46,650 --> 00:23:50,500
We'll focus mainly on client feedback first, then deadlines.

645
00:23:51,120 --> 00:23:51,520
Perfect.

646
00:23:51,700 --> 00:23:52,960
Thank you for clarifying.

647
00:23:52,980 --> 00:23:53,340
All right.

648
00:23:53,500 --> 00:23:58,320
So, as I mentioned, the client has requested some changes in the design layout.

649
00:23:58,620 --> 00:24:01,030
That might affect our development timeline.

650
00:24:01,690 --> 00:24:05,350
Yes, that's why I want input from the design and development teams.

651
00:24:06,090 --> 00:24:08,660
I think we should prioritize the mobile version first.

652
00:24:09,220 --> 00:24:10,180
That's a strong point.

653
00:24:10,660 --> 00:24:11,700
Any other suggestions?

654
00:24:12,460 --> 00:24:16,690
I agree, but we should also ensure performance doesn't drop with the new design.

655
00:24:17,290 --> 00:24:18,150
Good input.

656
00:24:18,170 --> 00:24:19,030
Let's note that down.

657
00:24:19,710 --> 00:24:22,810
Also, we may need to schedule a follow-up meeting midweek.

658
00:24:23,380 --> 00:24:26,660
Yes, let's touch base on Wednesday to review progress.

659
00:24:27,280 --> 00:24:27,940
Perfect.

660
00:24:28,270 --> 00:24:31,480
Before we move on, does anyone have anything urgent to add?

661
00:24:32,080 --> 00:24:32,400
Yes.

662
00:24:32,760 --> 00:24:35,220
I have a concern about the API integration.

663
00:24:35,440 --> 00:24:37,180
It's running slower than expected.

664
00:24:37,840 --> 00:24:38,600
That's important.

665
00:24:38,880 --> 00:24:40,070
Can you investigate it further?

666
00:24:40,640 --> 00:24:40,910
Yes.

667
00:24:41,190 --> 00:24:42,450
I'll prioritize it today.

668
00:24:43,050 --> 00:24:43,310
Great.

669
00:24:43,650 --> 00:24:44,130
Let's move on.

670
00:24:44,670 --> 00:24:48,510
Now let's shift to a one-on-one conversation between a manager and employee.

671
00:24:49,130 --> 00:24:49,670
Hi, Daniel.

672
00:24:49,850 --> 00:24:50,530
Do you have a minute?

673
00:24:51,100 --> 00:24:51,280
Sure.

674
00:24:51,490 --> 00:24:51,760
Come in.

675
00:24:52,320 --> 00:24:54,060
I wanted to talk about my workload.

676
00:24:54,340 --> 00:24:56,560
I feel like I'm a bit swamped this week.

677
00:24:57,010 --> 00:24:57,750
I understand.

678
00:24:58,270 --> 00:24:59,230
What's making it difficult?

679
00:24:59,820 --> 00:25:05,300
I have three deadlines overlapping, and I'm trying to prioritize them, but it's getting overwhelming.

680
00:25:05,900 --> 00:25:06,920
That makes sense.

681
00:25:06,940 --> 00:25:07,900
Let's go through them together.

682
00:25:08,300 --> 00:25:08,700
Thank you.

683
00:25:08,940 --> 00:25:10,290
I really need some clarity.

684
00:25:10,970 --> 00:25:11,290
Okay.

685
00:25:11,590 --> 00:25:14,310
First, the marketing report is highest priority.

686
00:25:14,590 --> 00:25:15,390
That's due Friday.

687
00:25:15,890 --> 00:25:16,250
Got it.

688
00:25:16,870 --> 00:25:19,880
Second, the internal review can be pushed to next Monday.

689
00:25:20,520 --> 00:25:21,520
That helps a lot.

690
00:25:21,730 --> 00:25:26,920
And third, the client presentation is important, but we can extend the preparation time.

691
00:25:27,440 --> 00:25:29,000
That relieves a lot of pressure.

692
00:25:29,040 --> 00:25:32,460
Remember, if you feel overloaded again, just tell me earlier.

693
00:25:33,030 --> 00:25:33,370
I will.

694
00:25:33,550 --> 00:25:34,750
I appreciate your support.

695
00:25:35,330 --> 00:25:35,850
No problem.

696
00:25:36,370 --> 00:25:38,770
Now let's move to email communication in the office.

697
00:25:39,330 --> 00:25:41,350
Hey, did you see the email from the client?

698
00:25:41,830 --> 00:25:42,510
Yes, I did.

699
00:25:42,870 --> 00:25:44,380
They're asking for an urgent update.

700
00:25:44,940 --> 00:25:47,120
I noticed they used ASAP.

701
00:25:47,320 --> 00:25:49,260
Do we need to respond immediately?

702
00:25:49,390 --> 00:25:53,520
Yes, ASAP means as soon as possible, ideally today.

703
00:25:54,120 --> 00:25:54,600
Understood.

704
00:25:54,840 --> 00:25:56,140
I'll draft a response now.

705
00:25:56,780 --> 00:25:57,020
Good.

706
00:25:57,340 --> 00:25:59,550
Make sure we CC the finance team as well.

707
00:26:00,090 --> 00:26:00,330
Right.

708
00:26:00,730 --> 00:26:03,810
By the way, I still sometimes forget what CC means.

709
00:26:04,510 --> 00:26:05,750
It means carbon compy.

710
00:26:06,090 --> 00:26:08,340
They get the email, but they don't need to reply.

711
00:26:08,920 --> 00:26:09,760
That's clear now.

712
00:26:10,460 --> 00:26:11,820
Also, keep the tone polite.

713
00:26:12,140 --> 00:26:14,400
Something like, thank you for your patience works well.

714
00:26:14,920 --> 00:26:15,290
Got it.

715
00:26:15,530 --> 00:26:16,550
I'll send it shortly.

716
00:26:16,850 --> 00:26:19,490
Now let's move to a team collaboration scenario.

717
00:26:19,990 --> 00:26:21,890
Hey, can I ask you for help with something?

718
00:26:22,470 --> 00:26:22,630
Sure.

719
00:26:22,780 --> 00:26:23,660
What's up?

720
00:26:23,680 --> 00:26:27,820
I'm working on this data report, but I think I'm missing some input from your side.

721
00:26:28,400 --> 00:26:29,280
OK. What do you need?

722
00:26:29,800 --> 00:26:32,850
I need clarification on the latest customer analysis.

723
00:26:33,830 --> 00:26:34,270
Ah, yes.

724
00:26:34,710 --> 00:26:37,870
That section compares customer satisfaction across regions.

725
00:26:38,490 --> 00:26:39,430
That makes sense now.

726
00:26:39,990 --> 00:26:41,900
I'll also send you the updated spreadsheet.

727
00:26:42,340 --> 00:26:44,020
That would be very helpful.

728
00:26:44,070 --> 00:26:44,590
No problem.

729
00:26:44,950 --> 00:26:46,630
Let's collaborate on the final version later.

730
00:26:47,170 --> 00:26:47,630
Sounds good.

731
00:26:48,230 --> 00:26:52,400
Now let's move to a slightly stressful situation, a mistake at work.

732
00:26:53,080 --> 00:26:56,300
Oh no, I think I sent the wrong file to the client.

733
00:26:56,920 --> 00:26:57,420
What happened?

734
00:26:57,960 --> 00:27:00,700
I sent the draft version instead of the final one.

735
00:27:01,390 --> 00:27:02,450
Okay, don't panic.

736
00:27:02,670 --> 00:27:03,390
We can fix this.

737
00:27:03,950 --> 00:27:06,030
I feel really embarrassed.

738
00:27:06,050 --> 00:27:06,590
It's okay.

739
00:27:06,750 --> 00:27:07,550
These things happen.

740
00:27:08,150 --> 00:27:09,540
What should I do now?

741
00:27:09,630 --> 00:27:11,560
Send a follow-up email immediately.

742
00:27:11,860 --> 00:27:13,920
Apologize and attach the correct file.

743
00:27:14,420 --> 00:27:16,080
Should I say sorry directly?

744
00:27:16,100 --> 00:27:19,730
Yes, something like, I apologize for the confusion.

745
00:27:20,290 --> 00:27:21,390
Okay, I'll do that now.

746
00:27:21,970 --> 00:27:23,070
And don't stress too much.

747
00:27:23,430 --> 00:27:25,710
One mistake doesn't define your performance.

748
00:27:25,730 --> 00:27:26,900
Thanks, I needed that.

749
00:27:27,500 --> 00:27:29,800
Now let's move to a client call scenario.

750
00:27:30,260 --> 00:27:33,180
Hello, this is Sarah from the project team speaking.

751
00:27:33,810 --> 00:27:36,190
Hi Sarah, this is Mr. Johnson from the client side.

752
00:27:36,690 --> 00:27:37,950
Thank you for joining the call.

753
00:27:38,250 --> 00:27:39,450
How can I help you today?

754
00:27:39,620 --> 00:27:42,080
We noticed some delays in the latest update.

755
00:27:42,680 --> 00:27:46,410
Yes, we're currently running behind schedule due to design changes.

756
00:27:47,090 --> 00:27:47,410
I see.

757
00:27:47,790 --> 00:27:48,970
Can you give us a new timeline?

758
00:27:49,510 --> 00:27:52,540
We expect to finalize everything by next Wednesday.

759
00:27:52,580 --> 00:27:55,740
That should be fine, but please keep us updated.

760
00:27:56,320 --> 00:27:56,720
Of course.

761
00:27:57,060 --> 00:27:58,860
I'll follow up with a detailed report.

762
00:27:59,500 --> 00:27:59,840
Thank you.

763
00:28:00,060 --> 00:28:00,880
That's appreciated.

764
00:28:01,450 --> 00:28:03,410
Now let's move to lunch break conversations.

765
00:28:04,050 --> 00:28:05,250
Finally, lunch time.

766
00:28:05,550 --> 00:28:06,570
I needed this break.

767
00:28:07,010 --> 00:28:07,750
Same here.

768
00:28:07,990 --> 00:28:09,630
I was completely swamped this morning.

769
00:28:10,110 --> 00:28:10,710
What are you having?

770
00:28:11,210 --> 00:28:12,270
Just a sandwich today.

771
00:28:12,580 --> 00:28:14,120
I didn't have time to prepare anything.

772
00:28:14,780 --> 00:28:16,320
I ordered from the cafe downstairs.

773
00:28:16,920 --> 00:28:17,280
Nice.

774
00:28:17,620 --> 00:28:18,780
Do you usually eat out?

775
00:28:19,360 --> 00:28:21,370
Sometimes, especially when I'm too busy.

776
00:28:21,950 --> 00:28:24,230
By the way, did you hear about Emma's promotion?

777
00:28:24,830 --> 00:28:26,270
Yes, she really deserves it.

778
00:28:26,530 --> 00:28:28,030
She always goes above and beyond.

779
00:28:28,070 --> 00:28:29,190
I agree.

780
00:28:29,430 --> 00:28:30,660
She's very dedicated.

781
00:28:30,680 --> 00:28:32,980
I think she'll be a great team leader.

782
00:28:33,560 --> 00:28:34,140
Definitely.

783
00:28:34,780 --> 00:28:36,860
Now let's talk about office small talk.

784
00:28:37,210 --> 00:28:38,540
Hey, how's your day going?

785
00:28:39,160 --> 00:28:40,280
Busy, but productive.

786
00:28:40,540 --> 00:28:41,000
How about you?

787
00:28:41,580 --> 00:28:41,860
Same.

788
00:28:42,200 --> 00:28:44,040
Back-to-back meetings all morning.

789
00:28:44,580 --> 00:28:45,810
That sounds exhausting.

790
00:28:46,330 --> 00:28:48,430
It is, but I try to stay organized.

791
00:28:49,070 --> 00:28:50,930
Do you have any plans after work?

792
00:28:50,950 --> 00:28:52,510
Just going home and relaxing.

793
00:28:52,800 --> 00:28:53,920
Maybe watching a series?

794
00:28:54,580 --> 00:28:55,300
Sounds perfect.

795
00:28:55,900 --> 00:28:58,360
Now let's move to a performance review meeting.

796
00:28:58,940 --> 00:28:59,940
Thanks for coming in today.

797
00:29:00,460 --> 00:29:00,870
Of course.

798
00:29:01,570 --> 00:29:04,050
Overall, your performance has been strong this quarter.

799
00:29:04,570 --> 00:29:04,970
Thank you.

800
00:29:04,990 --> 00:29:06,610
I really appreciate that.

801
00:29:06,900 --> 00:29:10,960
One area to improve is prioritizing tasks more efficiently.

802
00:29:11,580 --> 00:29:12,720
Yes, I agree.

803
00:29:12,780 --> 00:29:14,390
I sometimes struggle with that.

804
00:29:14,990 --> 00:29:16,750
Do you need any support or training?

805
00:29:17,270 --> 00:29:19,230
Maybe time management tools would help.

806
00:29:19,870 --> 00:29:20,630
We can arrange that.

807
00:29:21,070 --> 00:29:21,830
That would be great.

808
00:29:22,410 --> 00:29:23,180
Keep up the good work.

809
00:29:23,740 --> 00:29:24,800
Thank you for the feedback.

810
00:29:25,420 --> 00:29:27,280
Now let's move to the end of the workday.

811
00:29:27,920 --> 00:29:29,320
I think I'm going to head out now.

812
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:30,360
Same here.

813
00:29:30,610 --> 00:29:31,370
It's been a long day.

814
00:29:31,870 --> 00:29:33,370
Do you need anything before I leave?

815
00:29:34,030 --> 00:29:34,690
No, I'm good.

816
00:29:35,030 --> 00:29:36,110
We'll continue tomorrow.

817
00:29:36,340 --> 00:29:36,760
All right.

818
00:29:36,920 --> 00:29:37,550
See you tomorrow.

819
00:29:38,050 --> 00:29:38,330
See you.

820
00:29:38,610 --> 00:29:39,150
Have a good evening.

821
00:29:39,590 --> 00:29:39,910
You too.

822
00:29:40,570 --> 00:29:44,130
And that concludes our full-day Office English Conversation episode.

823
00:29:44,690 --> 00:29:53,380
Yes, we covered everything from morning greetings to meetings, emails, teamwork, mistakes, deadlines, and office communication.

824
00:29:54,000 --> 00:29:55,460
The key is practice.

825
00:29:55,480 --> 00:29:58,820
The more you hear and repeat these expressions, the more natural they become.

826
00:29:59,400 --> 00:30:00,850
Don't worry about perfection.

827
00:30:01,210 --> 00:30:02,550
Focus on communication.

828
00:30:03,130 --> 00:30:05,710
Thanks for listening, and we'll see you in the next episode.

829
00:30:05,990 --> 00:30:08,660
goodbye and keep practicing your English every day.

830
00:30:08,720 --> 00:30:16,950
Hello, everyone, and welcome back to the English at Work podcast, where we help you learn real, natural English used in everyday office life.

831
00:30:17,550 --> 00:30:18,090
Hi, everyone.

832
00:30:18,290 --> 00:30:19,380
Great to have you here again.

833
00:30:19,420 --> 00:30:24,940
Today's episode is a brand new continuation, Real English Conversations in an Office.

834
00:30:25,390 --> 00:30:31,600
We'll follow another full day inside an office with practical conversations you can actually use at work.

835
00:30:31,630 --> 00:30:32,270
Exactly.

836
00:30:32,670 --> 00:30:35,150
This episode is like a workplace simulation.

837
00:30:35,510 --> 00:30:42,040
You'll hear meetings, teamwork, emails, problem solving, casual office talk, and professional communication.

838
00:30:42,640 --> 00:30:50,530
And we'll also explain key phrases so you understand not only what people say, but why they say it that way in a real office environment.

839
00:30:51,430 --> 00:30:51,810
All right.

840
00:30:52,110 --> 00:30:53,580
Let's begin the day at the office.

841
00:30:54,520 --> 00:30:55,380
Good morning, everyone.

842
00:30:55,660 --> 00:30:56,960
You're early today, Jason.

843
00:30:57,170 --> 00:30:57,900
Good morning, Emma.

844
00:30:58,200 --> 00:31:00,660
Yes, I wanted to prepare for the project briefing.

845
00:31:01,060 --> 00:31:02,720
I think today is going to be a busy day.

846
00:31:03,240 --> 00:31:03,660
You're right.

847
00:31:04,020 --> 00:31:08,050
We have a client update meeting at 10 and a budget review after lunch.

848
00:31:08,070 --> 00:31:08,970
That sounds packed.

849
00:31:09,410 --> 00:31:11,910
Did you finish reviewing the client presentation slides?

850
00:31:12,820 --> 00:31:16,920
I did, but I think we need to refine a few points before showing them to the manager.

851
00:31:17,440 --> 00:31:17,920
Good idea.

852
00:31:18,400 --> 00:31:21,430
Refine here means improve small details, right?

853
00:31:21,950 --> 00:31:22,630
Exactly.

854
00:31:23,010 --> 00:31:27,350
It means making something better by adjusting details, not changing everything.

855
00:31:27,990 --> 00:31:28,330
Got it.

856
00:31:28,610 --> 00:31:30,380
Let's go through it together before the meeting.

857
00:31:30,960 --> 00:31:31,320
Perfect.

858
00:31:31,920 --> 00:31:32,460
All right, team.

859
00:31:32,700 --> 00:31:33,920
Let's start the morning briefing.

860
00:31:34,400 --> 00:31:35,620
First, project updates.

861
00:31:36,320 --> 00:31:38,380
The development team has made good progress.

862
00:31:38,680 --> 00:31:40,760
We are currently 85% complete.

863
00:31:41,330 --> 00:31:45,350
When you say good progress, what does that usually mean in office language?

864
00:31:46,170 --> 00:31:49,590
It means things are moving forward as expected without major problems.

865
00:31:50,230 --> 00:31:50,770
Understood.

866
00:31:51,260 --> 00:31:55,160
However, we are still facing a minor issue with the payment system integration.

867
00:31:55,720 --> 00:32:00,870
When someone says facing an issue, it sounds more professional than having a problem, right?

868
00:32:01,630 --> 00:32:02,070
Exactly.

869
00:32:02,490 --> 00:32:05,160
It softens the tone and sounds more business appropriate.

870
00:32:05,740 --> 00:32:07,220
What's the impact of this issue?

871
00:32:08,020 --> 00:32:11,960
It may delay testing by one or two days, but it won't affect the final launch date.

872
00:32:12,550 --> 00:32:13,210
That's good to hear.

873
00:32:13,910 --> 00:32:16,230
Now let's move to client communication updates.

874
00:32:16,970 --> 00:32:19,430
We received feedback from the client yesterday evening.

875
00:32:20,050 --> 00:32:20,890
What did they say?

876
00:32:21,470 --> 00:32:26,010
Overall, they are satisfied, but they requested a more user-friendly dashboard layout.

877
00:32:26,650 --> 00:32:29,280
When you say user-friendly, what does that mean?

878
00:32:29,320 --> 00:32:34,400
It means easy to use, simple to understand, and not confusing for users.

879
00:32:35,040 --> 00:32:35,340
Got it.

880
00:32:36,180 --> 00:32:38,530
They also asked for faster report generation.

881
00:32:39,170 --> 00:32:41,790
That will require optimization from the technical team.

882
00:32:42,490 --> 00:32:44,670
Yes, we will prioritize that this week.

883
00:32:45,260 --> 00:32:49,240
Prioritize means to focus on something first because it is important, right?

884
00:32:49,900 --> 00:32:50,560
Exactly.

885
00:32:50,850 --> 00:32:52,950
Let's move into the meeting room scenario.

886
00:32:53,650 --> 00:32:54,490
Good morning, everyone.

887
00:32:54,850 --> 00:32:56,970
Let's begin today's client presentation meeting.

888
00:32:57,610 --> 00:33:02,640
Before we start, I'd like to confirm, are we presenting the full version or just the prototype?

889
00:33:03,240 --> 00:33:03,840
Good question.

890
00:33:04,240 --> 00:33:05,960
We are presenting the prototype only.

891
00:33:06,540 --> 00:33:07,530
Thank you for clarifying.

892
00:33:08,010 --> 00:33:11,250
Clarifying means confirming something to avoid misunderstanding.

893
00:33:11,910 --> 00:33:12,370
Exactly.

894
00:33:12,750 --> 00:33:13,990
It's very common in meetings.

895
00:33:14,610 --> 00:33:14,910
All right.

896
00:33:15,120 --> 00:33:16,140
I'll start the presentation.

897
00:33:16,740 --> 00:33:17,580
Please go ahead.

898
00:33:17,980 --> 00:33:20,380
Slide one shows our project overview.

899
00:33:20,820 --> 00:33:24,390
The goal is to improve customer engagement through a digital platform.

900
00:33:25,250 --> 00:33:29,620
Sorry to interrupt, but could you explain what customer engagement means in simple terms?

901
00:33:30,220 --> 00:33:30,600
Of course.

902
00:33:31,100 --> 00:33:34,970
It means how customers interact with and respond to our product or service.

903
00:33:35,630 --> 00:33:36,270
That makes sense.

904
00:33:36,490 --> 00:33:36,850
Thank you.

905
00:33:37,470 --> 00:33:41,060
Moving on, slide two shows user growth over the last three months.

906
00:33:41,740 --> 00:33:42,820
That's impressive growth.

907
00:33:43,440 --> 00:33:46,280
Yes, but we still have challenges in retention.

908
00:33:46,460 --> 00:33:48,870
When you say retention, what does that refer to?

909
00:33:49,550 --> 00:33:53,010
It means keeping customers active over time instead of losing them.

910
00:33:53,710 --> 00:33:54,170
Understood.

911
00:33:54,880 --> 00:33:56,920
Now let's move to discussion and feedback.

912
00:33:57,800 --> 00:34:01,680
I think the design is strong, but we may need to simplify the navigation menu.

913
00:34:02,280 --> 00:34:03,240
That's a valid point.

914
00:34:03,610 --> 00:34:05,870
The current version might feel a bit complex.

915
00:34:06,650 --> 00:34:09,910
Valid point means a good or reasonable suggestion, right?

916
00:34:10,450 --> 00:34:12,780
Yes, it shows agreement or acknowledgement.

917
00:34:13,410 --> 00:34:16,880
I also think we should test it with real users before finalizing.

918
00:34:17,420 --> 00:34:18,200
Good suggestion.

919
00:34:18,480 --> 00:34:22,610
That's called user testing, which helps improve the product based on real feedback.

920
00:34:23,290 --> 00:34:23,710
Exactly.

921
00:34:24,350 --> 00:34:27,050
Now let's talk about disagreement in a professional way.

922
00:34:28,070 --> 00:34:31,960
I see your point, but I think we should launch earlier to stay ahead of competitors.

923
00:34:32,660 --> 00:34:34,160
I understand your perspective.

924
00:34:34,260 --> 00:34:37,150
However, launching too early might affect quality.

925
00:34:37,790 --> 00:34:39,170
That's a fair concern.

926
00:34:39,470 --> 00:34:44,890
In meetings, I understand your perspective, however, is a polite way to disagree, right?

927
00:34:45,540 --> 00:34:45,820
Yes.

928
00:34:46,240 --> 00:34:48,560
It keeps communication respectful and professional.

929
00:34:49,280 --> 00:34:50,680
Maybe we can meet halfway.

930
00:34:51,320 --> 00:34:52,140
What do you suggest?

931
00:34:52,760 --> 00:34:56,910
We could launch a beta version first and gather feedback before the full release.

932
00:34:57,610 --> 00:34:59,110
That sounds like a balanced approach.

933
00:34:59,750 --> 00:35:01,530
Now let's move to task assignment.

934
00:35:02,270 --> 00:35:04,930
We need clear action points before we end the meeting.

935
00:35:05,590 --> 00:35:08,820
Action points are tasks assigned after a meeting, right?

936
00:35:09,030 --> 00:35:09,560
Exactly.

937
00:35:09,980 --> 00:35:12,400
They ensure everyone knows what to do next.

938
00:35:13,120 --> 00:35:14,340
Okay, I'll summarize.

939
00:35:14,980 --> 00:35:15,340
Please do.

940
00:35:16,050 --> 00:35:19,450
First action point, design team will simplify the navigation.

941
00:35:20,310 --> 00:35:23,100
Second, development team will improve retention tracking.

942
00:35:23,700 --> 00:35:27,160
Third, marketing team will prepare the beta launch communication.

943
00:35:27,840 --> 00:35:28,160
Perfect.

944
00:35:28,820 --> 00:35:30,890
Now let's move to office problem solving.

945
00:35:31,770 --> 00:35:34,870
We've noticed a performance slowdown in the system this morning.

946
00:35:35,240 --> 00:35:39,670
When you say slow down, it means the system is not working as fast as usual.

947
00:35:40,350 --> 00:35:41,330
Yes, exactly.

948
00:35:41,930 --> 00:35:42,630
What's the cause?

949
00:35:43,510 --> 00:35:46,430
It appears to be related to server load during peak hours.

950
00:35:47,160 --> 00:35:48,280
So what's the solution?

951
00:35:49,120 --> 00:35:52,820
We're scaling up server capacity and optimizing background processes.

952
00:35:53,420 --> 00:35:53,920
That should help.

953
00:35:54,590 --> 00:35:56,610
Now let's move to informal office talk.

954
00:35:57,490 --> 00:35:58,810
Finally, coffee break.

955
00:35:59,410 --> 00:36:00,930
Yes, I really needed this.

956
00:36:01,370 --> 00:36:02,730
I've been swamped all morning.

957
00:36:03,090 --> 00:36:05,730
Swamped means extremely busy, right?

958
00:36:06,310 --> 00:36:06,850
Exactly.

959
00:36:06,990 --> 00:36:08,110
Too much work at once.

960
00:36:08,950 --> 00:36:10,520
Did you hear about the new team lead?

961
00:36:11,140 --> 00:36:11,320
No.

962
00:36:11,400 --> 00:36:11,880
What happened?

963
00:36:12,580 --> 00:36:13,580
Daniel got promoted.

964
00:36:14,140 --> 00:36:15,050
That's great news.

965
00:36:15,170 --> 00:36:16,070
He really deserves it.

966
00:36:16,790 --> 00:36:18,650
He always goes above and beyond.

967
00:36:19,270 --> 00:36:21,080
That means he does more than expected.

968
00:36:21,780 --> 00:36:22,280
Exactly.

969
00:36:22,900 --> 00:36:25,040
Now let's talk about emails and communication.

970
00:36:25,930 --> 00:36:28,250
I just sent a follow-up email to the client.

971
00:36:28,750 --> 00:36:33,880
Follow-up email means a message sent after a previous email to continue communication, right?

972
00:36:34,520 --> 00:36:34,780
Yes.

973
00:36:35,440 --> 00:36:36,060
What did you write?

974
00:36:36,960 --> 00:36:39,450
I wrote a polite reminder about the pending approval.

975
00:36:40,050 --> 00:36:40,250
Good.

976
00:36:40,790 --> 00:36:43,650
In business emails, being polite is very important.

977
00:36:44,390 --> 00:36:45,090
Absolutely.

978
00:36:45,730 --> 00:36:48,120
Now let's simulate end-of-day conversation.

979
00:36:49,060 --> 00:36:50,560
I think I'm going to wrap up for today.

980
00:36:51,160 --> 00:36:51,540
Same here.

981
00:36:51,840 --> 00:36:52,800
It's been a productive day.

982
00:36:53,660 --> 00:36:55,110
Did we complete all action points?

983
00:36:55,730 --> 00:36:55,990
Yes.

984
00:36:56,210 --> 00:36:57,090
Everything is on track.

985
00:36:57,640 --> 00:36:57,940
Great.

986
00:36:58,220 --> 00:36:59,480
I'll update the manager tomorrow.

987
00:37:00,020 --> 00:37:00,380
Perfect.

988
00:37:00,590 --> 00:37:01,120
See you tomorrow.

989
00:37:01,660 --> 00:37:01,950
See you.

990
00:37:02,150 --> 00:37:02,710
Have a good evening.

991
00:37:03,330 --> 00:37:08,050
And that brings us to the end of today's episode, Real English Conversations in an Office.

992
00:37:08,810 --> 00:37:17,700
Today we practiced full office communication, meetings, updates, disagreements, presentations, emails, teamwork, and problem solving.

993
00:37:18,300 --> 00:37:19,700
The key takeaway is simple.

994
00:37:20,220 --> 00:37:23,020
Office English is not about complicated vocabulary.

995
00:37:23,400 --> 00:37:26,690
It's about clear, polite, and structured communication.

996
00:37:27,010 --> 00:37:32,030
If you practice these conversations regularly, you will naturally start speaking more confidently at work.

997
00:37:32,570 --> 00:37:34,750
Thanks for listening, and we'll see you in the next episode.

998
00:37:35,530 --> 00:37:37,910
Goodbye, and keep practicing your English every day.

999
00:37:38,340 --> 00:37:42,260
Hello, everyone, and welcome back to our English Learning Podcast.

1000
00:37:42,700 --> 00:37:43,320
I'm Daniel.

1001
00:37:43,820 --> 00:37:44,760
And I'm Olivia.

1002
00:37:45,100 --> 00:37:47,120
Thank you so much for joining us today.

1003
00:37:47,560 --> 00:37:51,430
Today we have another exciting and practical episode for you.

1004
00:37:51,790 --> 00:37:52,370
That's right.

1005
00:37:52,610 --> 00:37:56,690
Today's topic is Real English Conversations in an Office.

1006
00:37:56,790 --> 00:38:02,860
In this episode, you'll hear natural English conversations that people really use at work every day.

1007
00:38:03,240 --> 00:38:15,810
We'll talk about office situations like arriving at work, speaking with coworkers, handling meetings, solving problems, answering emails, talking to managers, and even chatting during lunch breaks.

1008
00:38:16,370 --> 00:38:23,340
These conversations are very useful if you work in an office or if you want to work in an international company someday.

1009
00:38:23,400 --> 00:38:28,970
And don't worry, we'll use simple and clear English so you can follow along easily.

1010
00:38:29,450 --> 00:38:35,340
All right, everyone, let's begin today's podcast, Real English Conversations in an Office.

1011
00:38:35,760 --> 00:38:36,500
Good morning, Emma.

1012
00:38:36,680 --> 00:38:37,820
You're here early today.

1013
00:38:38,280 --> 00:38:39,030
Morning, Ryan.

1014
00:38:39,270 --> 00:38:42,330
Yeah, I wanted to finish a few things before the meeting starts.

1015
00:38:42,730 --> 00:38:43,410
That's smart.

1016
00:38:43,630 --> 00:38:45,620
Mondays are always busy around here.

1017
00:38:46,040 --> 00:38:46,940
Tell me about it.

1018
00:38:47,120 --> 00:38:49,860
My inbox already has over 50 emails.

1019
00:38:50,050 --> 00:38:50,830
Same here.

1020
00:38:51,330 --> 00:38:54,330
I spent almost an hour replying to clients this morning.

1021
00:38:54,770 --> 00:38:58,800
Sometimes I feel like answering emails is a full-time job by itself.

1022
00:38:59,320 --> 00:39:00,620
Honestly, I agree.

1023
00:39:00,660 --> 00:39:04,980
And every time I clear my inbox, five more emails arrive.

1024
00:39:05,480 --> 00:39:06,120
Exactly.

1025
00:39:06,140 --> 00:39:07,140
It never ends.

1026
00:39:07,610 --> 00:39:10,870
By the way, did you finish the sales report for today's meeting?

1027
00:39:11,330 --> 00:39:11,750
Almost.

1028
00:39:12,010 --> 00:39:14,770
I just need to update the final numbers from last week.

1029
00:39:15,050 --> 00:39:15,420
Great.

1030
00:39:15,700 --> 00:39:20,800
The manager said the report is very important because the directors want detailed updates this month.

1031
00:39:21,220 --> 00:39:22,010
I heard about that.

1032
00:39:22,390 --> 00:39:24,310
Everyone seems a little stressed lately.

1033
00:39:24,730 --> 00:39:27,690
Yeah, but I think it's because the company is growing quickly.

1034
00:39:28,030 --> 00:39:28,660
That's true.

1035
00:39:29,010 --> 00:39:32,760
Growth is good, but it also means more pressure and responsibilities.

1036
00:39:33,180 --> 00:39:33,760
Definitely.

1037
00:39:34,240 --> 00:39:36,370
Excuse me, Sophia, do you have a minute?

1038
00:39:36,770 --> 00:39:37,430
Sure, Kevin.

1039
00:39:37,690 --> 00:39:38,370
What's up?

1040
00:39:38,390 --> 00:39:40,870
I'm having trouble understanding this spreadsheet.

1041
00:39:41,270 --> 00:39:42,610
Which part is confusing?

1042
00:39:42,830 --> 00:39:45,010
The financial forecast section.

1043
00:39:45,280 --> 00:39:47,900
I'm not sure how to calculate the projected revenue.

1044
00:39:48,480 --> 00:39:49,640
Oh, I can help with that.

1045
00:39:50,060 --> 00:39:54,750
The system automatically estimates future sales based on current performance trends.

1046
00:39:55,310 --> 00:39:55,690
I see.

1047
00:39:56,110 --> 00:39:58,390
So these numbers aren't manually entered?

1048
00:39:58,890 --> 00:39:59,090
No.

1049
00:39:59,320 --> 00:40:01,360
The software generates them automatically.

1050
00:40:01,720 --> 00:40:03,340
That makes much more sense now.

1051
00:40:03,580 --> 00:40:04,540
Thanks for explaining it.

1052
00:40:04,880 --> 00:40:05,430
No problem.

1053
00:40:05,860 --> 00:40:08,570
It confused me at first, too, when I joined the company.

1054
00:40:08,720 --> 00:40:11,820
Honestly, there are still many systems I'm trying to learn.

1055
00:40:12,200 --> 00:40:13,520
That's completely normal.

1056
00:40:13,980 --> 00:40:16,340
New employees usually need time to adjust.

1057
00:40:16,840 --> 00:40:18,110
I appreciate your patience.

1058
00:40:18,590 --> 00:40:19,030
Anytime.

1059
00:40:19,490 --> 00:40:22,090
Don't hesitate to ask questions if you need help.

1060
00:40:22,110 --> 00:40:22,870
All right, everyone.

1061
00:40:22,930 --> 00:40:25,110
Let's begin the weekly marketing meeting.

1062
00:40:25,550 --> 00:40:26,010
Good morning.

1063
00:40:26,390 --> 00:40:29,840
First, I'd like to review last week's campaign performance.

1064
00:40:30,360 --> 00:40:33,800
Overall, social media engagement improved by 15%.

1065
00:40:34,240 --> 00:40:35,820
That's excellent news.

1066
00:40:36,360 --> 00:40:38,080
What contributed to the increase?

1067
00:40:38,270 --> 00:40:42,310
Short video content performed especially well with younger audiences.

1068
00:40:42,850 --> 00:40:43,350
Interesting.

1069
00:40:43,790 --> 00:40:46,240
Did any platform perform better than the others?

1070
00:40:46,660 --> 00:40:46,960
Yes.

1071
00:40:47,260 --> 00:40:50,620
Instagram and TikTok generated the highest engagement levels.

1072
00:40:51,100 --> 00:40:51,840
That makes sense.

1073
00:40:52,340 --> 00:40:54,770
Short videos are extremely popular right now.

1074
00:40:55,190 --> 00:40:55,730
Exactly.

1075
00:40:56,030 --> 00:40:59,190
People prefer fast and entertaining content nowadays.

1076
00:40:59,630 --> 00:41:02,400
Did we receive customer feedback about the campaign?

1077
00:41:02,760 --> 00:41:03,060
Yes.

1078
00:41:03,420 --> 00:41:07,780
Most responses were positive, especially regarding the product demonstrations.

1079
00:41:08,030 --> 00:41:08,990
That's great to hear.

1080
00:41:09,050 --> 00:41:13,660
Customers usually trust products more when they can see real demonstrations.

1081
00:41:14,160 --> 00:41:14,760
Absolutely.

1082
00:41:15,080 --> 00:41:18,530
Visual content helps customers understand products better.

1083
00:41:19,050 --> 00:41:19,430
All right.

1084
00:41:19,650 --> 00:41:23,390
Let's continue focusing on video marketing for next month as well.

1085
00:41:23,730 --> 00:41:24,190
Sounds good.

1086
00:41:24,590 --> 00:41:26,780
Hey, Julia, are you free for lunch today?

1087
00:41:27,160 --> 00:41:28,080
Yeah, I think so.

1088
00:41:28,660 --> 00:41:29,480
Where do you want to go?

1089
00:41:29,500 --> 00:41:32,340
A new cafe opened near the office last week.

1090
00:41:32,590 --> 00:41:34,230
I heard the food is really good.

1091
00:41:34,410 --> 00:41:36,350
Oh, I know the place you're talking about.

1092
00:41:36,730 --> 00:41:38,780
Some people from accounting went there yesterday.

1093
00:41:38,800 --> 00:41:39,720
Did they like it?

1094
00:41:40,060 --> 00:41:42,100
Apparently, the sandwiches were amazing.

1095
00:41:42,500 --> 00:41:43,060
Perfect.

1096
00:41:43,260 --> 00:41:44,480
I'm starving already.

1097
00:41:44,520 --> 00:41:45,560
Same here.

1098
00:41:45,620 --> 00:41:48,410
I skipped breakfast this morning because I overslept.

1099
00:41:48,750 --> 00:41:50,670
That's dangerous on a busy workday.

1100
00:41:50,690 --> 00:41:51,590
I know.

1101
00:41:51,630 --> 00:41:53,370
I've been tired all week.

1102
00:41:53,810 --> 00:41:55,000
You should get more rest.

1103
00:41:55,360 --> 00:41:57,640
Working while exhausted is never productive.

1104
00:41:58,060 --> 00:41:58,340
True.

1105
00:41:58,740 --> 00:42:01,890
Sometimes I stay up too late answering emails from home.

1106
00:42:02,020 --> 00:42:04,980
Honestly, work-life balance is really important.

1107
00:42:05,480 --> 00:42:08,070
I completely agree, but it's hard sometimes.

1108
00:42:08,550 --> 00:42:09,130
Definitely.

1109
00:42:09,170 --> 00:42:12,270
Modern office life can become stressful very quickly.

1110
00:42:12,790 --> 00:42:15,000
Oh no, I think I made a mistake.

1111
00:42:15,520 --> 00:42:16,100
What happened?

1112
00:42:16,540 --> 00:42:21,680
I accidentally sent the unfinished presentation to the client instead of the final version.

1113
00:42:22,270 --> 00:42:23,570
Ah, that's frustrating.

1114
00:42:23,950 --> 00:42:25,350
I feel terrible right now.

1115
00:42:25,750 --> 00:42:26,530
Don't panic.

1116
00:42:26,830 --> 00:42:28,300
Mistakes happen to everyone.

1117
00:42:28,660 --> 00:42:30,760
But this client is very important.

1118
00:42:30,890 --> 00:42:34,320
Then fix the problem quickly and communicate honestly.

1119
00:42:34,700 --> 00:42:35,260
You're right.

1120
00:42:35,580 --> 00:42:39,400
I should send the correct file immediately and apologize professionally.

1121
00:42:39,780 --> 00:42:40,240
Exactly.

1122
00:42:40,740 --> 00:42:44,230
Most clients appreciate honesty and fast communication.

1123
00:42:44,610 --> 00:42:46,510
I just hope they won't think I'm careless.

1124
00:42:47,110 --> 00:42:49,620
One mistake doesn't define your professionalism.

1125
00:42:49,980 --> 00:42:51,500
You're usually very organized.

1126
00:42:51,880 --> 00:42:52,260
Thanks.

1127
00:42:52,580 --> 00:42:54,100
That actually makes me feel better.

1128
00:42:54,480 --> 00:42:54,720
Good.

1129
00:42:55,100 --> 00:42:58,570
Now take a deep breath and solve the issue step by step.

1130
00:42:58,820 --> 00:43:00,980
All right, I'm sending the email now.

1131
00:43:01,360 --> 00:43:02,440
Good afternoon, everyone.

1132
00:43:02,740 --> 00:43:05,790
Today, I'd like to introduce our new employee, Ethan.

1133
00:43:06,170 --> 00:43:06,870
Hi, everyone.

1134
00:43:07,130 --> 00:43:07,910
Nice to meet you all.

1135
00:43:08,370 --> 00:43:11,490
Ethan recently joined our customer support department.

1136
00:43:11,510 --> 00:43:13,120
I'm really excited to work here.

1137
00:43:13,500 --> 00:43:15,560
Everyone has been very welcoming so far.

1138
00:43:15,900 --> 00:43:16,740
That's great to hear.

1139
00:43:16,800 --> 00:43:20,950
Honestly, I was nervous this morning because it's my first office job.

1140
00:43:21,270 --> 00:43:21,870
Don't worry.

1141
00:43:22,190 --> 00:43:24,170
Everyone feels nervous on their first day.

1142
00:43:24,190 --> 00:43:24,830
Thanks.

1143
00:43:25,210 --> 00:43:27,800
I'm still trying to remember everyone's names.

1144
00:43:28,010 --> 00:43:28,850
That takes time.

1145
00:43:28,890 --> 00:43:29,810
You'll learn quickly.

1146
00:43:30,290 --> 00:43:30,910
I hope so.

1147
00:43:31,360 --> 00:43:33,840
The office seems much bigger than I expected.

1148
00:43:34,220 --> 00:43:36,440
Yeah, it can feel overwhelming at first.

1149
00:43:36,840 --> 00:43:38,640
By the way, where's the meeting room?

1150
00:43:38,960 --> 00:43:40,300
I don't want to get lost later.

1151
00:43:40,680 --> 00:43:44,110
Just walk down the hallway and turn left near the coffee machine.

1152
00:43:44,430 --> 00:43:44,790
Got it.

1153
00:43:45,010 --> 00:43:45,370
Thank you.

1154
00:43:45,790 --> 00:43:46,390
No problem.

1155
00:43:46,870 --> 00:43:48,850
And if you need help with anything, just ask.

1156
00:43:49,290 --> 00:43:50,140
I appreciate that.

1157
00:43:50,590 --> 00:43:52,860
Excuse me, Mr. Walker, do you have a moment?

1158
00:43:53,200 --> 00:43:53,920
Of course, Lily.

1159
00:43:54,180 --> 00:43:54,700
Please come in.

1160
00:43:55,200 --> 00:43:57,460
I wanted to discuss my workload with you.

1161
00:43:57,700 --> 00:43:59,680
All right, what seems to be the issue?

1162
00:44:00,140 --> 00:44:06,870
Recently, I've been handling multiple projects at the same time, and I'm struggling to manage all the deadlines effectively.

1163
00:44:07,330 --> 00:44:08,010
I understand.

1164
00:44:08,330 --> 00:44:11,790
You've definitely been taking on a lot of responsibilities lately.

1165
00:44:11,830 --> 00:44:17,560
I'm trying my best, but I'm worried the quality of my work might suffer if I continue at this pace.

1166
00:44:18,000 --> 00:44:22,280
That's an important conversation to have before burnout becomes a bigger issue.

1167
00:44:22,320 --> 00:44:23,290
Exactly.

1168
00:44:23,310 --> 00:44:26,250
I didn't want to wait until things became overwhelming.

1169
00:44:26,420 --> 00:44:27,800
That was a smart decision.

1170
00:44:28,240 --> 00:44:31,970
Communication is extremely important in professional environments.

1171
00:44:32,390 --> 00:44:46,990
Thank you.
